WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.085 --> 00:00:18.249
At that moment the instruments in the gallery indicated that only a thin sheet of ice separated us

2
00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:19.009
from open water.

3
00:00:19.829 --> 00:00:21.590
I was desperate to breathe some fresh air.

4
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:27.112
Once again I heard the sound of the water tanks filling, but this time it was only the

5
00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:27.992
tanks in the stern.

6
00:00:29.108 --> 00:00:34.312
As the bow of the submarine began to rise, I realized that the Nautilus was going to attempt

7
00:00:34.352 --> 00:00:35.893
to break through the ice above us.

8
00:00:36.914 --> 00:00:41.897
The submarine attacked the ice with its battering ram, and then it backed off and rushed forward again.

9
00:00:42.898 --> 00:00:46.100
This time the icy crust gave way and we shot to the surface.

10
00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:52.164
Someone tore open the hatches and pure air came rushing into all parts of the Nautilus.

11
00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:57.128
I have no idea how I got to the platform; perhaps Ned carried me there.

12
00:00:58.028 --> 00:01:01.209
But once I was out in the fresh sea air, I breathed deeply.

13
00:01:01.229 --> 00:01:03.910
"How delightful this air is!"

14
00:01:04.371 --> 00:01:05.011
said Conseil.

15
00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:09.693
"And we may all breathe freely because there is enough for everyone."

16
00:01:10.813 --> 00:01:15.975
Ned did not say anything but inhaled deeply, opening his jaws wide enough to frighten a shark.

17
00:01:16.956 --> 00:01:22.058
When our strength returned, I tried to express my gratitude to Ned and Conseil for saving my life.

18
00:01:22.078 --> 00:01:28.597
"My friends," I said, "we will be bonded by this experience forever, and I have a great obligation

19
00:01:28.637 --> 00:01:29.137
to you both."

20
00:01:29.157 --> 00:01:33.139
"Which I will take advantage of," Ned said slyly.

21
00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.919
"What do you mean?"

22
00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:36.120
asked Conseil.

23
00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:41.682
"Only that I will insist on taking the professor and you with me when I leave this infernal

24
00:01:41.762 --> 00:01:47.645
submarine." "Well," said Conseil, "after all this, are we heading in the right direction?"

25
00:01:47.665 --> 00:01:52.086
"Yes, because we're definitely headed north," I replied.

26
00:01:52.106 --> 00:01:58.314
"But it remains to be seen," said Ned, "whether Captain Nemo will take us into the Atlantic or

27
00:01:58.354 --> 00:01:59.235
the Pacific Ocean.

28
00:01:59.616 --> 00:02:04.520
In other words, are we going toward seas that are heavily traveled or seas that are lonely?"

29
00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:09.605
I could not answer that question and feared that Captain Nemo would prefer to take us to the

30
00:02:09.625 --> 00:02:13.388
Pacific Ocean, so that he could complete his underwater tour of the world.

31
00:02:14.329 --> 00:02:18.433
But by March 31 it was clear that we were heading into the Atlantic.

32
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:25.498
By April 12 we had crossed the equator and were within twenty miles of Guiana, a French territory

33
00:02:25.518 --> 00:02:28.621
that would have given refuge to Ned, Conseil, and me.

34
00:02:29.521 --> 00:02:33.804
But a stiff breeze was blowing and the waves would have capsized the small boat.

35
00:02:34.845 --> 00:02:37.767
Ned must have realized this because he made no mention of escaping.

36
00:02:38.127 --> 00:02:40.008
For my part, I said nothing either.

37
00:02:41.469 --> 00:02:44.772
I didn't want to encourage Ned in an attempt to escape that would fail.

38
00:02:45.752 --> 00:02:51.016
To Ned's great disappointment, the Nautilus stayed out of the Caribbean Sea and the Gulf of Mexico.

39
00:02:52.064 --> 00:02:55.726
He had hoped either to land on one of the islands or to hail one of the many

40
00:02:55.806 --> 00:02:57.486
boats passing from island to island.

41
00:02:58.507 --> 00:03:03.489
That assumed, of course, that he was able to launch the small boat without Captain Nemo's knowledge.

42
00:03:04.509 --> 00:03:08.871
On April 16 we sighted Martinique and Guadeloupe from a distance.

43
00:03:09.872 --> 00:03:15.234
We had been prisoners on the Nautilus for five months and had traveled seventeen thousand leagues.

44
00:03:15.254 --> 00:03:18.275
"This voyage will never come to an end!"

45
00:03:18.695 --> 00:03:20.236
Ned moaned one day in despair.

46
00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.402
"We're going to have to be the ones who end it because that captain never will."

47
00:03:26.403 --> 00:03:31.466
Meanwhile what concerned me was that Captain Nemo had become more serious and less sociable.

48
00:03:32.887 --> 00:03:34.147
I rarely saw him anymore.

49
00:03:34.968 --> 00:03:40.211
He used to spend time explaining underwater marvels to me; these days he left me to my studies

50
00:03:40.271 --> 00:03:41.711
and never came into the gallery.

51
00:03:42.832 --> 00:03:44.233
My attitude had changed too.

52
00:03:45.093 --> 00:03:50.168
I realized that I now had the research to write a solid book about underwater life, and I

53
00:03:50.208 --> 00:03:52.770
wanted to get back to land to see that book published.

54
00:03:53.791 --> 00:03:57.113
On April 20 we were cruising underwater near the Bahamas.

55
00:03:58.114 --> 00:04:02.377
Tall cliffs rose beneath the sea, their surfaces covered with weeds.

56
00:04:03.538 --> 00:04:06.239
I was looking out the gallery windows with Conseil and Ned.

57
00:04:06.259 --> 00:04:10.322
"Giant squid could live in some of those caves," I suggested.

58
00:04:10.342 --> 00:04:14.805
"Oh, I'll never believe that such animals exist," said Ned.

59
00:04:14.826 --> 00:04:21.765
"Well," Conseil said very seriously, "I remember seeing a large vessel being pulled under the waves by a

60
00:04:21.785 --> 00:04:22.526
squid's tentacles."

61
00:04:24.086 --> 00:04:28.368
"Oh, really," Ned said skeptically, "and exactly where was that?"

62
00:04:28.388 --> 00:04:31.530
"In a church in Brittany," said Conseil.

63
00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.791
"There's a painting showing that scene."

64
00:04:33.811 --> 00:04:38.072
"He's right," I said as Ned and I turned away from the windows.

65
00:04:39.153 --> 00:04:43.555
"The painting, however, is based merely on a legend about a squid that was supposed to be a

66
00:04:43.635 --> 00:04:46.236
mile long, more like an island than an animal.

67
00:04:46.748 --> 00:04:51.730
Another legend tells of a bishop building an altar on a large rock; after the bishop said mass,

68
00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:55.512
the rock—which was a giant squid—walked into the sea."

69
00:04:55.532 --> 00:04:58.153
"How much of those stories is true?"

70
00:04:58.473 --> 00:04:59.154
asked Conseil.

71
00:04:59.174 --> 00:05:03.896
"When it comes to sea monsters, imaginations run wild," I replied.

72
00:05:04.856 --> 00:05:08.037
"The legends are untrue, but huge squids do exist.

73
00:05:08.458 --> 00:05:13.660
Some naturalists have calculated that a six-foot squid would have tentacles twenty-seven feet long."

74
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:19.603
Ned was especially interested in how someone would catch one of these creatures, so I told him about

75
00:05:19.643 --> 00:05:22.685
an unsuccessful attempt that occurred a few years before.

76
00:05:22.705 --> 00:05:24.967
"How big was this squid?"

77
00:05:25.287 --> 00:05:25.767
asked Ned.

78
00:05:25.787 --> 00:05:28.529
"Wasn't it about 18 feet?"

79
00:05:28.869 --> 00:05:29.530
said Conseil.

80
00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:34.613
"And when it waved its tentacles about, didn't they look like a nest of serpents?

81
00:05:35.394 --> 00:05:37.295
And didn't it have a beak like a parrot's?"

82
00:05:38.356 --> 00:05:39.997
"That's all correct," I said.

83
00:05:40.017 --> 00:05:46.537
"Well, then, I think it—or its brother—is right outside the window!

84
00:05:47.157 --> 00:05:53.040
Look!" Ned and I rushed to the glass, where we saw an immense squid that had to be

85
00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.662
at least twenty-five feet long and four thousand pounds.

86
00:05:57.723 --> 00:05:58.924
What a freak of nature!

87
00:05:59.764 --> 00:06:05.427
It had hundreds of suckers along its tentacles and its beaklike mouth was lined with rows of sharp

88
00:06:05.527 --> 00:06:08.068
teeth. Nearby were more squid!

89
00:06:09.449 --> 00:06:11.470
I counted seven by the light of the Nautilus.

90
00:06:12.551 --> 00:06:14.332
Suddenly the submarine stopped.

91
00:06:15.132 --> 00:06:17.593
A shock made it tremble from bow to stern.

92
00:06:17.613 --> 00:06:21.515
"We can't have hit anything because we're still floating," said Ned.

93
00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:26.197
Captain Nemo and his lieutenant came into the gallery and looked through the windows.

94
00:06:26.217 --> 00:06:30.519
"The squid have disabled our propeller," Captain Nemo explained.

95
00:06:30.539 --> 00:06:34.540
"We're going to rise to the surface and see if we can get rid of them."

96
00:06:35.601 --> 00:06:40.103
As the submarine rose, we followed Nemo up the stairs that led to the platform.

97
00:06:41.183 --> 00:06:45.741
There, about ten men were waiting, armed with hatchets and ready to attack.

98
00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:49.762
Conseil and I picked up hatchets while Ned seized a harpoon.

99
00:06:50.703 --> 00:06:53.584
One of the crewmen unscrewed the bolts of the hatch and opened it.

100
00:06:54.965 --> 00:06:58.406
Immediately a huge tentacle slid down and grabbed the man.

101
00:06:58.426 --> 00:07:00.627
"Help! Help!"

102
00:07:01.147 --> 00:07:02.047
he cried in French.

103
00:07:03.068 --> 00:07:07.170
I had a fellow countryman on board, and I hadn't even known it until this awful moment!

104
00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:12.712
Captain Nemo and his crew fought the squid, but it blinded them with a stream of its black

105
00:07:12.752 --> 00:07:19.118
liquid. By the time the men could see again, the squid had disappeared overboard with the unfortunate crewman.

106
00:07:20.178 --> 00:07:24.180
Ten or twelve squid now climbed up the sides and platform of the Nautilus.

107
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:29.962
Ned fought them off with his harpoon until one pinned him to the platform with its horrible, heavy

108
00:07:30.022 --> 00:07:30.462
tentacles.
